var SaltCore=SaltCore||{};SaltCore["journey-deity-maat"]=(()=>{var o=Object.defineProperty;var s=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var h=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var d=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var l=(t,e)=>{for(var n in e)o(t,n,{get:e[n],enumerable:!0})},u=(t,e,n,i)=>{if(e&&typeof e=="object"||typeof e=="function")for(let a of h(e))!d.call(t,a)&&a!==n&&o(t,a,{get:()=>e[a],enumerable:!(i=s(e,a))||i.enumerable});return t};var f=t=>u(o({},"__esModule",{value:!0}),t);var c={};l(c,{journey:()=>r});var r={id:"deity-maat",kind:"deity",paths:["egyptian"],deityRef:"traditional/deity/maat",title:"Maat: The Feather and the Order",summary:"A month with Maat, the goddess with the ostrich feather and the order of the world she stands for: truth, justice and balance kept in daily acts. Study her in the creation texts, the teachings of the sages and the weighing of the heart, learn how kings offered her to the gods, keep ten evenings of honest reckoning, and decide whether to dedicate.",welcome:`This journey spends about a lunar month with Maat, who is two things at once: a goddess, a woman crowned with an ostrich feather, and maat, the right order of the world that she embodies. Egyptians did not keep these apart. To honour the goddess was to live in maat, and to live in maat was to honour her. She suits people who care about truth and fairness, people who judge, teach, mediate, keep accounts or make rules, people trying to set their own lives in order, and anyone who wants a devotion that is mostly about how they behave.

You will learn where she stands in the Egyptian creation texts, how the sages taught maat as a way of life, the weighing of the heart and the declarations of the dead, how kings presented her to the gods, and how she is honoured today, along with what is ancient and what is modern in that. For ten evenings you will weigh your day honestly before her. At the end you may dedicate yourself to her, or not.

This is a self-dedication. It is not an initiation into Kemetic Orthodoxy or any other group, and it does not replace one. It will ask more of your conduct than of your shrine.`,about:`Maat (also written Ma'at) was the Egyptian goddess and concept of truth, justice, balance and the right order of the cosmos. Her name is written with a sign that may show a plinth or measuring base, often with an ostrich feather, and she is pictured as a seated or standing woman with that feather on her head, sometimes with wings. Her opposite was isfet: falsehood, violence, disorder. In the creation texts she is the daughter of the creator, the breath of his life; from the New Kingdom she is called the daughter of Ra, and she stands in the prow of his sun boat. The gods were said to "live on maat", and the king's first duty was to "set maat in place of isfet". In the judgement of the dead the heart was weighed against her feather, in the Hall of the Two Truths. Judges were called priests of Maat, and the tomb-builders of Deir el-Medina lived in "the Place of Truth". Her own temples were few, but every temple offered her: from the New Kingdom on, the king presented a small figure of Maat to the gods as the offering that contained all others. Today she is honoured in Kemetic religion, where living in maat is the centre of daily practice, in African-centred thought as a model of ethics, and by many Pagans as a goddess of truth and justice.`,notes:['The "42 Laws of Maat", "42 Principles" or "42 Ideals of Maat" sold as commandments are a modern rewording of the declarations of innocence in Book of the Dead chapter 125. In Egypt these were statements spoken by the dead before the judges, not a list of rules, and the wording varied between papyri.',"Maat is both a goddess and a principle. Honouring her means living truthfully and fairly, not only making offerings. A beautiful shrine kept by someone who lies and cheats would have made no sense to an Egyptian.","Egyptian maat was not modern equality or human rights. It was an order in which kings, officials, families and gods all had places, and it could justify harsh things too, such as the punishment of enemies. Honour her honestly, without pretending she was a modern liberal ideal.",'Many associations for Maat (particular crystals, Libra, the "feather of truth" as a modern spiritual symbol) are modern. They are fine to use if you know they are modern.',"Kemetic religion is a living practice with its own communities and customs, and African-centred readings of maat are important to many people of African descent. Learn from them, and ask before joining their spaces.","Feathers: buy only farmed ostrich feathers or use a drawn or paper feather. Never take feathers from wild birds of prey or protected species; in many countries it is illegal."],holyDays:[],stages:[{kind:"threshold",title:"The Feather",intro:"Begin by learning who Maat is and making her a plain, orderly shrine with a feather. The threshold ends with a short introduction rite: you greet her, give water, bread and incense, and say one true thing about yourself.",days:3,lessons:[{id:"maat-who",title:"Goddess and Order",summary:"Her name and feather, maat and isfet, her place beside the creator, and why she is both a goddess and a way of living.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The woman with the feather",body:`Maat is shown as a woman, usually seated with her knees drawn up or standing, wearing a close sheath dress and a band around her head with a single tall ostrich feather in it. Sometimes she has wings spread from her arms, like Isis. Very often she is small: a little seated figure held in the king's hand, or a tiny goddess crouched on top of the balance in the judgement scene.

The ostrich feather on its own could stand for her, and in writing it can spell her name. Why the ostrich feather is not certain. Feathers are light and evenly balanced either side of the quill, and the god Shu, the air, wore the same feather; Maat and Shu are brother and sister in some texts. A modern devotee can let the feather say what it seems to say: something light, true and balanced.`},{heading:"Maat and isfet",body:`The word maat has no single English equivalent. Translators use truth, justice, order, balance, rightness or righteousness, and each catches only part of it. It is the way things should be: the sun rising, the flood arriving, the king ruling fairly, the scribe keeping honest accounts, the neighbour returning what he borrowed, the strong not crushing the weak.

Its opposite is isfet: lies, violence, greed, chaos. Isfet was not a distant evil. It crept in whenever people cheated, quarrelled, neglected the gods or let the poor go hungry. Maat had to be done, again and again, in small things, or the world would slip back toward disorder. That is why Egyptians spoke of "doing maat" and "speaking maat": it is something you do, not only something you believe.`},{heading:"Daughter of the creator",body:`In the Coffin Texts, spells written inside Middle Kingdom coffins around 2000 BCE, the creator Atum floats alone in the primeval waters, weary and inert. The waters, Nun, tell him to breathe in his daughter Maat and hold her to his nose, so that his heart may live. In the same text his two children are named as Life and Maat. Order is the breath of the creator: without it, even the god cannot live.

From the New Kingdom she is called the daughter of Ra, the sun god. She stands in the prow of his boat as it crosses the sky, and hymns say that Ra lives on maat, eats maat and drinks maat. Some texts make Thoth, the god of writing and reckoning, her husband or companion, which fits: accurate records and honest speech are how maat is kept.`},{heading:"Few temples, every temple",body:`Maat had few temples of her own. A small temple of Maat stood within the precinct of Montu at Karnak, rebuilt by several kings, and there were chapels for her elsewhere. But she was present in every temple and every court. Judges were called priests of Maat. Later Greek writers say that the chief judge wore an image of Truth on a gold chain around his neck; Egyptian statues of officials sometimes show a small figure of Maat worn as a pendant.

Kings put her in their names. Hatshepsut was Maatkare, "Maat is the ka of Ra"; Amenhotep III was Nebmaatre, "Ra is lord of maat"; Ramesses II was Usermaatre, "the maat of Ra is strong". Akhenaten called himself "living in maat". To rule at all, a king had to claim that he kept her.`}],knowAndDont:{known:'Her iconography with the ostrich feather, the pairing of maat and isfet, her role as daughter of the creator in the Coffin Texts (spell 80) and of Ra in New Kingdom hymns, her place in the sun boat, royal names compounded with maat, and the title "priest of Maat" for judges are all well attested.',unknown:"The origin of her name and of the feather is uncertain, and so is how the goddess and the concept first related to each other. How far ordinary people prayed to Maat as a goddess, rather than speaking of maat as an ideal, is hard to say."},inPractice:"Choose a name (Maat or Ma\u2019at) and an image: the seated goddess with her feather, or a single feather, farmed or drawn. Learn the two words maat and isfet. Before you sleep each night this week, name one moment of each from your day, without judging yourself harshly.",reflect:["Which English word comes closest to maat for you: truth, justice, order, balance? Which one is hardest for you to live?","Where in your life right now is a small piece of isfet that you could put right?"],further:["Richard H. Wilkinson, The Complete Gods and Goddesses of Ancient Egypt (2003), entry on Maat","Emily Teeter, The Presentation of Maat: Ritual and Legitimacy in Ancient Egypt (1997)"],library:["traditional/deity/maat","traditional/deity/ra","traditional/deity/thoth","traditional/crystal/clear_quartz"]}],practices:[{id:"maat-shrine",title:"Set up a plain, orderly shrine",instructions:`Choose a shelf or small table you can keep level, clean and tidy; for Maat, order is itself an offering. Use a spirit level if you have one, and smile at yourself if you do. Wipe it with water that has a pinch of natron, washing soda or salt dissolved in it.

Lay a clean white cloth. Set her image at the back, or stand a single feather upright in a small holder. In front, place a bowl for water, a dish for bread, an incense burner on a heatproof tile, and a white or gold candle. Add, if you like, a small pair of scales, clear quartz or lapis lazuli, and a notebook for her.

Keep nothing on her shrine that is broken, borrowed and not returned, or there for show. Cover her image with a cloth between visits if you can, as Egyptian shrines were closed when the god was not being served.`,times:1,prompt:"What did you set on her shrine, and what did you have to put in order to make room for it?"}],rite:{title:"Introduction to Maat",summary:"A short first visit: purify, open the shrine, greet her, offer water, bread and incense, and say one true thing about yourself.",minutes:20,ingredients:[{name:"Maat",ref:"traditional/deity/maat"},{name:"An offering bowl of fresh, cool water",ref:"traditional/vessel/offering_bowl"},{name:"A pinch of natron, washing soda or salt in a cup of water, for washing"},{name:"A piece of bread",ref:"traditional/vessel/dish"},{name:"Frankincense, myrrh or cedar incense",ref:"traditional/herb/frankincense"},{name:"An incense burner on a heatproof tile",ref:"traditional/tool/incense_burner"},{name:"A white or gold candle",ref:"traditional/candle/white"},{name:"A feather, farmed or drawn on paper"}],preparation:"Wash your hands and face and rinse your mouth with the natron or salt water, spitting it out. Put on clean clothes. Tidy the room a little. Have the shrine clean and her image covered. Turn off your phone.",steps:[{title:"Light and purify",instructions:"Light the candle, then the incense. Touch a drop of the natron water to your lips and pass your hands through the smoke.",light:!0,spoken:"I am pure. My hands are washed and my mouth is clean. I come before the lady of truth."},{title:"Open the shrine",instructions:"Uncover her image, or set the feather upright before you.",spoken:"Your face is uncovered, Maat. Awake in peace, daughter of Ra."},{title:"Adore",instructions:"Raise both hands, palms toward her, at the height of your face, and speak.",spoken:"Homage to you, Maat, daughter of Ra, breath of the creator, lady of the feather. You on whom the gods live, you who stand in the prow of the boat: I greet you."},{title:"Offer",instructions:"Lift the water, then the bread, toward her and set them down. Hold the incense up for a moment.",spoken:"An offering for Maat: cool water, bread and sweet smoke. I give them freely and honestly. May they please you."},{title:"Say one true thing",instructions:"Speak plainly: your name, why you have come, and one true thing about yourself that you do not often say aloud. It need not be a confession; it only needs to be true. Tell her you will spend a month learning before you decide anything. Then sit quietly.",minutes:5},{title:"Close and withdraw",instructions:"Thank her. Cover the shrine and snuff the candle. Step back before you turn away.",snuff:!0,spoken:"Go in peace, Maat. I will come again."}],closing:"Eat the bread later; that is the reversion of offerings, and nothing is wasted. Pour the water on a plant or the earth. Write in your journal the true thing you said.",safety:"Keep candle and incense on a heatproof surface in a ventilated room, away from curtains, children, pets and pet birds, and never leave them burning unattended. Keep the feather well away from the flame. Do not swallow the natron water."}},{kind:"study",title:"The Way of Maat",intro:"Three lessons: maat in the teachings of the sages and the duties of kings; the weighing of the heart and the declarations of the dead; and the Presentation of Maat, the offering that contained every other. Keep a short morning greeting at her shrine in between.",days:11,lessons:[{id:"maat-teachings",title:"Speak Maat, Do Maat",summary:"Maat in the wisdom texts, in the duties of the king and the official, and in an ancient complaint about justice denied.",minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"The teachings of the sages",body:`Egyptians wrote down advice for living well in texts called sebayt, "teachings", usually framed as a father instructing his son. The most famous, the Teaching of Ptahhotep, is set in the Old Kingdom and survives in Middle Kingdom copies. It says, in a plain rendering: great is maat, lasting in its effect; it has not been disturbed since the time of Osiris. Ptahhotep warns against greed, gossip and arrogance, tells officials to listen patiently to petitioners, and reminds the powerful that wisdom can be found among the women at the grindstones.

The Teaching for King Merikare advises a ruler to do maat while he is on earth, to comfort the weeper, not to oppress the widow, and not to take a man from his father's property. These are not laws in our sense. They are a picture of a decent person in a society with steep ranks: honest, restrained, generous downward and loyal upward.`},{heading:"The Eloquent Peasant",body:`A Middle Kingdom story, the Tale of the Eloquent Peasant, is the great Egyptian text about justice. A peasant on his way to market is robbed by an official's underling. He appeals to the high steward, and speaks so well about maat that the steward, on the king's orders, keeps him talking through nine long petitions, feeding his family in secret, so that his speeches can be written down. Only at the end is he given justice.

The petitions are passionate. In a plain rendering, the peasant tells the steward: speak maat, do maat, for it is mighty, it is great, it endures. He calls the official a balance that has tipped, a rudder that steers wrong, a pilot who lets the ship sink. The story both celebrates maat and admits how slow the powerful are to keep it. Egyptians knew the gap between the ideal and the courts.`},{heading:"Maat in place of isfet",body:`The king's first task was to "set maat in place of isfet". The phrase appears in the Pyramid Texts and in royal inscriptions for two thousand years. After the upheaval of Akhenaten's reign, the Restoration Stela of Tutankhamun describes a land in disorder, its temples neglected and its gods turned away, and the young king setting maat back in her place.

Below the king, officials boasted in their tomb inscriptions of having done maat: I gave bread to the hungry, water to the thirsty, clothes to the naked, a boat to the one who had none; I judged two parties so that both went out satisfied. These "autobiographies" are self-praise, of course, but they show what people wanted to be remembered for.`},{heading:"Maat as a web",body:`The German Egyptologist Jan Assmann described maat as "connective justice": the threads of give and take, speaking and listening, remembering and reciprocity that bind people to one another and to the gods. In his reading, isfet is above all forgetting, greed and the refusal to answer for what you have received.

You do not have to accept every part of a modern scholar's theory. But it is a useful way to think about a devotion to Maat. It is not mainly private purity. It is how you treat the people your life is tied to, and whether the web is stronger or weaker for your being in it.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"The Teachings of Ptahhotep and for Merikare, the Tale of the Eloquent Peasant, the royal formula of setting maat in place of isfet, Tutankhamun\u2019s Restoration Stela and the ideal autobiographies of officials are all well attested.",unknown:'Who really wrote the wisdom texts, and how widely ordinary people knew them, is uncertain; they were copied by scribes in schools. How closely real courts followed these ideals is unclear, and the Eloquent Peasant suggests not always. Assmann\u2019s "connective justice" is a modern interpretation, influential but debated.'},inPractice:"Read the Tale of the Eloquent Peasant and a few maxims of Ptahhotep in a modern translation (Lichtheim\u2019s is clear). Copy one line onto a card for her shrine. This week, choose one place where you hold a little power, at work, at home, in a group, and use it fairly and on purpose.",reflect:["Where are you the steward, holding power over someone else\u2019s petition? Where are you the peasant?","Which thread in your own web of give and take has gone slack?"],further:["Miriam Lichtheim, Ancient Egyptian Literature, vol. 1: The Old and Middle Kingdoms (1973), for Ptahhotep, Merikare and the Eloquent Peasant","R. B. Parkinson, The Tale of Sinuhe and Other Ancient Egyptian Poems (1997), for a modern translation of the Eloquent Peasant","Jan Assmann, The Mind of Egypt (2002), on maat as connective justice"],library:["traditional/deity/maat","traditional/deity/thoth","traditional/crystal/sodalite"]},{id:"maat-judgement",title:"The Hall of the Two Truths",summary:'The weighing of the heart against her feather, the declarations of the dead, and what the modern "42 Laws" really are.',minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"The weighing",body:`The Book of the Dead is a collection of spells copied on papyrus for burials from about 1550 BCE. Its chapter 125 takes the dead person into the Hall of the Two Truths, the double Maat, before Osiris and the divine judges. In the famous vignette, the heart is laid on one pan of a balance and the feather of Maat on the other. Anubis checks the plumb line, Thoth records the result, and Ammit the devourer waits for a heart that fails. Sometimes a small figure of Maat herself sits on the top of the scale, or she stands to welcome the dead.

The heart, for Egyptians, was the seat of thought, memory and character. A heart in balance with the feather was one whose life had been lived in maat. It is one of the most familiar images from Egypt, and it is easy to forget how demanding it is: the dead are weighed against truth itself.`},{heading:"The declarations of innocence",body:`Before the weighing, the dead person declares that they have not done a long list of wrongs: once to the great god, and once to forty-two judges, each addressed by name. In the Papyrus of Nu, as Budge translated it (1899), the list begins "I have not committed sin" and includes "I have not uttered falsehood" and "I have not made another person to weep." Others deny robbery, murder, cheating with weights and measures, stealing offerings, damming water that belonged to others, and eavesdropping.

These declarations are often called the Negative Confession, though they deny rather than confess. The list varied between papyri. It was a statement the dead hoped to make truthfully, not a code handed down from a mountain.`},{heading:"The 42 Laws are modern",body:`You will find, on posters, websites and jewellery, the "42 Laws of Maat", "42 Principles" or "42 Ideals", usually rewritten in the positive ("I honour the sacred", "I am truthful") and presented as ancient commandments. They are modern adaptations of chapter 125, made in the twentieth and twenty-first centuries. Some are thoughtful and many people use them sincerely as a guide.

It is worth knowing the difference. The ancient list was spoken at death, in the negative, with spells added in case the heart decided to speak against its owner. Book of the Dead chapter 30B is exactly that: a plea to the heart not to stand up as a witness. Egyptians were honest enough to hedge.`},{heading:"A judgement you can practise now",body:`The judgement scene can sound frightening. Egyptians treated it practically: they lived as well as they could, made their declarations, and trusted in the gods and their spells. For a modern devotee of Maat, the hall is best understood as a daily practice rather than a final exam. At the end of each day you can weigh it: where was I truthful, where was I fair, where did I tip the scale.

That is what the ten evenings of this journey will ask. Not perfection, which no Egyptian expected, but an honest weighing and a small correction, every day.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Book of the Dead chapters 125 and 30B, the judgement vignette in many papyri (Ani, Hunefer, Nu and others), the declarations before the forty-two judges and the variations between copies are well documented.",unknown:"How literally Egyptians expected the judgement to happen, and how they reconciled it with protective spells, is beyond our reach. Why there were forty-two judges is uncertain; the number is often linked with the nomes, the provinces of Egypt. The modern 42 Laws have no single author."},inPractice:'Read chapter 125 in a good translation, or study the judgement scene in the Papyrus of Ani. Then write your own short list of declarations: true sentences you could say tonight, beginning "I have not". Keep it on her shrine. If you use a version of the 42 Laws, note on it that it is modern.',reflect:["Which of the ancient declarations could you say truthfully today? Which could you not, yet?","Would you rather be weighed once at the end, or every evening? Why?"],further:["Raymond O. Faulkner (trans.), The Egyptian Book of the Dead: The Book of Going Forth by Day (1994), with the Papyrus of Ani","E. A. Wallis Budge, Egyptian Ideas of the Future Life (1899), Project Gutenberg","John H. Taylor (ed.), Journey through the Afterlife: Ancient Egyptian Book of the Dead (British Museum, 2010)"],library:["traditional/deity/maat","traditional/deity/osiris","traditional/deity/anubis","traditional/deity/thoth"]},{id:"maat-presentation",title:"I Bring You Maat",summary:"The royal offering of a small figure of Maat, the Place of Truth at Deir el-Medina, and what offering a principle means.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"The offering of all offerings",body:`On the walls of New Kingdom and later temples, the king kneels or stands before a god holding out on his palm a tiny seated figure of Maat with her feather. The accompanying words are some form of "I bring you Maat" or "I give you Maat". Emily Teeter's study of these scenes shows that the presentation of Maat became one of the most important temple offerings, and that it was understood to sum up all the others: bread, beer, incense and linen were all forms of maat given back to the gods who lived on it.

The offering said two things at once. It fed the god, and it declared that the king was keeping order, which was why he had the right to stand in the temple at all. Some kings' names were written inside the little figure, so that the king offered both the goddess and his own claim to rule.`},{heading:"The Place of Truth",body:`The craftsmen who cut and decorated the royal tombs in the Valley of the Kings lived in a walled village now called Deir el-Medina. Their institution was called the Place of Truth, Set-Maat, and they called themselves servants in the Place of Truth. Thousands of their notes, accounts and letters survive on potsherds and papyri.

They show maat in ordinary life: absences from work recorded with reasons (a sick wife, a scorpion sting, brewing beer), donkey hire disputes argued before a local court, oaths sworn by the king, and prayers of men who confess they have sinned and ask a god for mercy. It is not a perfect village. It is a village that kept records, argued its cases, and tried.`},{heading:"Offering a principle",body:`How do you give someone an idea? The Egyptian answer was: by giving them a figure of it, and then living it. The little Maat on the king's palm was an object; the order it stood for had to be kept outside the temple. The same was true for officials who wore her image and for scribes who wrote "true of voice" after the names of the blessed dead.

That gives a modern devotee two kinds of offering. One is the figure: a feather, a drawn or carved Maat, a written statement of what you will keep. The other is the life: a true account, a debt paid, a quarrel settled fairly. The first means little without the second.`},{heading:"True of voice",body:`The blessed dead were called maa-kheru, "true of voice" or "justified": their words had been found true before the judges. The phrase follows the names of the dead on countless coffins and stelae, and it was used of Osiris and of Horus when the court of the gods found for them.

It is a beautiful aim to hold. Not famous, not powerful, not even happy, but true of voice: someone whose words matched their deeds.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"The presentation of Maat in temple reliefs from the New Kingdom to the Roman period is documented in detail by Teeter. Deir el-Medina, its title Set-Maat, its records and its local court are among the best-known communities of the ancient world. Maa-kheru is standard after the names of the dead.",unknown:"When the presentation of Maat began and how it was performed in real ritual, as opposed to reliefs, is not fully known. How far the Deir el-Medina workers thought of their title as religious, rather than administrative, is uncertain."},inPractice:"Make a small figure of Maat or a paper feather for her shrine, the way the king held one out. This week, look for one record you keep (accounts, notes, messages) and make it honest and complete. Settle one small debt or promise you have left hanging.",reflect:['What would it take for you to be "true of voice", your words matching your deeds?',"What does it mean to you to offer a principle rather than a thing?"],further:["Emily Teeter, The Presentation of Maat: Ritual and Legitimacy in Ancient Egypt (1997)","Leonard H. Lesko (ed.), Pharaoh\u2019s Workers: The Villagers of Deir el Medina (1994)","A. G. McDowell, Village Life in Ancient Egypt: Laundry Lists and Love Songs (1999)"],library:["traditional/deity/maat","traditional/vessel/offering_bowl","traditional/crystal/lapis_lazuli"],premade:["folk-thoth-offering"]}],practices:[{id:"maat-morning-water",title:"Morning water and an intention",instructions:"Each morning, wash your hands and face, uncover her shrine, pour fresh water into her bowl, raise your hands and say a line of greeting. Then name one thing you will do fairly or truthfully today. Pour yesterday\u2019s water on a plant. Cover the shrine.",times:11,every:"day",prompt:"One line: what did you mean to do in maat today, and did you?"}]},{kind:"practice",title:"Ten Evenings of the Feather",intro:"For ten evenings, one Egyptian ten-day week, you weigh your day before Maat: an offering, an honest reckoning and one small correction. Read the lesson on honouring her today before you begin.",days:10,lessons:[{id:"maat-today",title:"Maat Today: Practice, Days and Care",summary:"Modern communities and readings of maat, how to offer, her days, and the sensitive points.",minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"Who honours her now",body:`In Kemetic religion, the modern revival of Egyptian religion, living in maat is the centre of practice for almost everyone, whatever gods they serve. Reconstructionists build ethics from the wisdom texts and keep a figure of Maat or a feather on their shrines. Kemetic Orthodoxy, a temple founded in 1988 by Tamara Siuda and based in the United States, teaches maat as the basis of its members' conduct and honours the goddess in its calendar; its members follow that temple's customs.

Maat is also central to African-centred thought. The scholar Maulana Karenga, in Maat, the Moral Ideal in Ancient Egypt (2004), reads maat as an African ethical tradition with lessons for the present, and many people of African descent find in her a source of pride and moral guidance. Outside these, Pagans and witches of many paths honour Maat as a goddess of truth, justice and balance, and lawyers, judges and mediators of many faiths keep a small feather on their desks.`},{heading:"What to offer and how",body:`Water, bread and incense, given in the Egyptian shape: purify, open, adore, offer, close, and afterwards the reversion of offerings, eating or sharing the bread. Frankincense, myrrh and cedar suit her. A feather, a small figure of Maat or a written statement of what you will keep are traditional in spirit, after the royal presentation.

But the main offering is conduct. Telling the truth when it costs you. Returning what you borrowed. Paying people fairly and on time. Listening to both sides before you decide. Correcting a mistake you made, publicly if it was public. Each of these can be named at her shrine: "I bring you Maat."`},{heading:"Her days",body:`No festival of Maat herself is securely known, and it is honest to say so. She was offered every day in every temple, and her feast, in a sense, was the daily rite. Many Kemetics renew their commitment to living in maat at Wep Ronpet, the new year, when the order of the world is renewed; that is a modern custom built on an ancient idea. The epagomenal days before it, the dangerous days "upon the year", are a natural time to weigh the year that is ending.

The feast of Thoth, around the 19th of the first month, suits her as his companion, with Plutarch's honey, fig and "a sweet thing is Truth". Many devotees also keep a small weighing on the evening of each new or full moon. Choose one calendar and keep it from year to year.`},{heading:"Sensitive points",body:`The first is honesty about her history. Maat was the order of a kingdom with slaves, rigid ranks and royal violence against enemies; the same word that protected the widow justified the smiting of foreigners. You can honour her truly without pretending otherwise.

The second is self-judgement. A devotion built on weighing the heart can turn into scrupulosity, harsh self-criticism or perfectionism. Egyptians did not expect anyone to be flawless; they wrote spells to quiet an accusing heart. If your evening reckoning leaves you anxious or ashamed rather than steadier, shorten it, be kind to yourself, and talk to someone you trust or a professional. If you are in crisis, call your local emergency number or a crisis line (988 in the US, Samaritans on 116 123 in the UK and Ireland).

The third is using Maat against others. She is not a weapon for judging other people's lives or for winning arguments. The scale weighs your own heart.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Kemetic Orthodoxy and other Kemetic communities are documented modern movements; Karenga\u2019s book is a major African-centred study. The daily offering of Maat in temples, the feast of Thoth and Plutarch\u2019s honey-and-fig custom are attested.",unknown:"No festival of Maat is securely known, and the Wep Ronpet custom is modern. There is no single modern Kemetic calendar, and ancient dates drifted against the seasons. How Egyptians held the ideal of maat together with slavery and royal violence is debated by scholars."},inPractice:"Fix your offering habit: water each morning, a weighing each evening, bread and incense once a week, a fuller offering at the new year. Choose one standing act of maat to offer her, such as never letting a day end with a lie you have not corrected. Decide in advance how you will stop the evening reckoning from becoming self-punishment.",reflect:["Which part of your conduct do you most want to bring into balance this year?","How will you weigh your day honestly without weighing yourself down?"],further:["Maulana Karenga, Maat, the Moral Ideal in Ancient Egypt: A Study in Classical African Ethics (2004)","Richard J. Reidy, Eternal Egypt: Ancient Rituals for the Modern World (2010)"],library:["traditional/deity/maat","traditional/crystal/jade","traditional/candle/gold","traditional/herb/cedar"],premade:["folk-gratitude-ritual"]}],practices:[{id:"maat-ten-evenings",title:"Ten evenings of the feather",instructions:`Each evening: wash your hands and face, uncover the shrine, light the candle and incense, raise your hands and greet her. Give water and bread. Then weigh the day: write one place where you kept maat and one where you tipped the scale. For the second, write one small, practical correction you will make tomorrow. Read both aloud to her, thank her, cover the shrine and snuff the flame. Eat the bread tomorrow.

Each evening also has a theme to think about while you write. Evening 1: the feather; something light and true. Evening 2: maat and isfet; a small disorder you could mend. Evening 3: the breath of the creator; what keeps you alive and steady. Evening 4: Ptahhotep; listening fully to someone. Evening 5: the Eloquent Peasant; a justice you are owed or owe. Evening 6: bread to the hungry; one act of generosity. Evening 7: the declarations; write three honest sentences beginning "I have not". Evening 8: the heart that might accuse you; forgive yourself one thing. Evening 9: the Place of Truth; an honest record. Evening 10: true of voice; what you would like your words and deeds to say together.

If you miss an evening, carry on where you left off. Keep the reckoning short: ten minutes is plenty.`,times:10,every:"day",prompt:"Where did you keep maat today, where did you tip the scale, and what will you correct tomorrow?"}]},{kind:"discernment",title:"The Two Truths",intro:"Take a few days with only the morning water. Read your journal from the beginning and weigh the month as Maat would: fairly, without flattery and without cruelty. Dedication is one good outcome; so is waiting, stopping, or finding that another god, perhaps Thoth, Osiris or Ra, is the one calling you.",days:3,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"maat-reread",title:"Weigh the month",instructions:"Read your journal from the first entry in one sitting. On one side of a page, write what has grown more truthful or fair in your life this month; on the other, what has not changed. Lay the feather between them. Then answer the questions below.",times:1,prompt:"Honestly weighed, what has this month with Maat changed?"}],questions:["Are you drawn to Maat the goddess, to maat the way of living, or to both? Can you say which at her shrine?","Has the evening reckoning made you steadier and kinder, or harsher with yourself? What would keep it healthy for a year?","Can you keep a small, steady practice (water, the weighing, acts of fairness) through an ordinary year, including the weeks when you are not proud of yourself?","Would a Kemetic community, or practical work for fairness and justice where you live, serve what you are looking for better than a self-dedication?","If you decide to wait or stop, can you thank her, give a last offering and take down the shrine in good order? That is a good ending too."]},{kind:"dedication",title:"The Dedication",intro:"If you choose to go on, this is your self-dedication to Maat: a promise you write and speak yourself, and above all a promise about how you will live. It is not an initiation into Kemetic Orthodoxy or any other group, and it does not replace one. Wep Ronpet, the feast of Thoth, a new or full moon, or the day after you have put something important right are all fitting.",days:1,lessons:[],practices:[],rite:{title:"Self-Dedication to Maat",summary:"A full rite in the Egyptian shape: purification, opening, adoration, offering, your own declarations, the vow, the presentation of Maat, a time of stillness, the reversion and closing.",minutes:45,ingredients:[{name:"Maat",ref:"traditional/deity/maat"},{name:"An offering bowl of fresh water",ref:"traditional/vessel/offering_bowl"},{name:"Natron, washing soda or salt in a cup of water, for washing"},{name:"Bread, and a drink",ref:"traditional/vessel/dish"},{name:"Frankincense or cedar and an incense burner",ref:"traditional/herb/frankincense"},{name:"A white or gold candle",ref:"traditional/candle/gold"},{name:"A feather, or a small figure of Maat you have made or drawn"},{name:"Your own list of declarations, written by hand"},{name:"Your vow, written by hand"},{name:"Clear quartz or lapis lazuli to keep on the shrine",ref:"traditional/crystal/clear_quartz",optional:!0}],preparation:`During the day, write two things. First, your declarations: a short list of true sentences beginning "I have not", in the manner of chapter 125, things you can honestly say. Second, your vow: what you will do (for example, morning water, the evening weighing, one act of fairness each week, correcting your mistakes promptly), for how long (a year and a day is common), and what you ask of her. Keep both short and true.

Before the rite, put one thing in your home or life in order. Then bathe, rinse your mouth with natron or salt water, and dress in clean, preferably white clothes. Clean the shrine and set everything out, with her image covered.`,steps:[{title:"Purify",instructions:"Light the candle and the incense. Wash your hands in the natron water, touch it to your lips, and pass your hands through the smoke.",light:!0,spoken:"I am pure, I am pure. I have washed, I have passed through the smoke. I come before Maat with clean hands and a true mouth."},{title:"Open the shrine",instructions:"Uncover her image. Set the feather or the figure of Maat beside it.",spoken:"The doors are opened. Your face is uncovered, Maat, lady of truth. Awake in peace."},{title:"Adore",instructions:"Raise both hands, palms toward her, and speak the praise slowly.",spoken:"Homage to you, Maat, daughter of Ra, breath in the nose of the creator, lady of the feather, lady of the Hall of the Two Truths. You on whom the gods live, you who stand in the prow of the boat, you against whom every heart is weighed: I praise you."},{title:"Offer",instructions:"Lift the water, the bread and the drink toward her and set them down.",spoken:"An offering for Maat: water and bread, drink and sweet smoke, every good and pure thing on which a goddess lives. May it please you."},{title:"Your declarations",instructions:"Read your own list of declarations aloud, slowly. Say only what is true. If you hesitate over one, leave it out.",minutes:4,spoken:"These are my words, spoken before you. I have tried to say only what is true."},{title:"Speak your vow",instructions:"Stand and read your vow aloud, clearly. Sign and date it.",spoken:"This is my word, weighed and freely given. I will keep it as well as I am able, and when I fail I will set it right. Hear me, Maat."},{title:"Present Maat",instructions:"Hold the feather or the little figure of Maat on your open palm and lift it toward her image, as the kings did on the temple walls. Then lay it on your vow.",spoken:"I bring you Maat. I give you Maat. Let my words and my deeds be one, and let me be true of voice."},{title:"Sit with her",instructions:"Sit quietly before her. Notice whether your heart feels heavy or light, without judging it.",minutes:8},{title:"Reversion and closing",instructions:"Thank her. Eat a piece of the bread. Leave the vow with the feather on her shrine. Cover the shrine and snuff the candle. Step back before you turn away.",snuff:!0,spoken:"What you have received, I share. Go in peace, Maat. I am yours, and I will keep your order."}],closing:"Pour the water on the earth. Keep the vow and the feather on her shrine and reread the vow at each new moon. Record the rite in your journal, and the next day, do one thing that keeps the vow.",safety:"Keep flame and incense on heatproof surfaces in a ventilated room, away from curtains, children, pets and pet birds, and never leave them burning unattended. Keep the feather and paper well away from the flame. Do not drink alcohol if you should not; water and juice are fine offerings."}},{kind:"ongoing",title:"Living in Maat",intro:"After the dedication, the practice is mostly your life: water in the morning, the day weighed in the evening, fair acts offered, the new year kept, and a yearly renewal of your vow.",days:365,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"maat-daily",title:"Water and the weighing",instructions:"Most mornings, give fresh water and name one thing you will do in maat. Most evenings, weigh the day in a line or two: where you kept the balance, and what you will correct. On hard days, one true sentence is enough.",times:365,every:"day"},{id:"maat-weekly",title:"A weekly offering of Maat",instructions:'Once a week (or once in every ten days, the Egyptian week), give bread and incense, eat the bread afterwards, and present to her one fair or truthful act from that week by name: "I bring you Maat".',times:52,every:"week",prompt:"What did you bring her this week?"},{id:"maat-holy-days",title:"Her days",instructions:`Keep the epagomenal days at the end of the Egyptian year as a time to weigh the whole year: reread your journal, settle what you can, and write down what you could not. At Wep Ronpet, the new year, renew your commitment to living in maat with a fuller offering and a new list of declarations. At the feast of Thoth, eat honey and a fig and say "A sweet thing is Truth".

Use the calendar you have chosen, and keep it from year to year.`,times:2,every:"holy-day",prompt:"How did you weigh the year, and what will you set right in the next?"},{id:"maat-renewal",title:"Yearly rededication",instructions:"On the anniversary of your dedication or at Wep Ronpet, read the year\u2019s journal, write new declarations and a new vow, and repeat the dedication rite. Change the promise honestly. If you are ending the dedication, thank her, give a last offering and take down the shrine in good order.",times:1,prompt:"What has a year of weighing your days taught you about your own heart?"}]}],sources:['Book of the Dead ch. 125 (Papyrus of Nu), trans. E. A. Wallis Budge, Egyptian Ideas of the Future Life (1899), Project Gutenberg ebook 11277: "I have not committed sin", "I have not uttered falsehood", "I have not made another person to weep"',"Coffin Texts spell 80, on Maat as the breath and daughter of Atum, trans. R. O. Faulkner, The Ancient Egyptian Coffin Texts (1973\u201378)","The Teaching of Ptahhotep, the Teaching for King Merikare and the Tale of the Eloquent Peasant, in Miriam Lichtheim, Ancient Egyptian Literature, vol. 1 (1973); R. B. Parkinson, The Tale of Sinuhe and Other Ancient Egyptian Poems (1997)","Emily Teeter, The Presentation of Maat: Ritual and Legitimacy in Ancient Egypt (1997)","Jan Assmann, Ma\u2019at: Gerechtigkeit und Unsterblichkeit im Alten \xC4gypten (1990) and The Mind of Egypt (2002)","Diodorus Siculus, Library of History 1.75, trans. C. H. Oldfather (Loeb, 1933; public domain), LacusCurtius, on the chief judge\u2019s image of Truth","Leonard H. Lesko (ed.), Pharaoh\u2019s Workers (1994); A. G. McDowell, Village Life in Ancient Egypt (1999), on Deir el-Medina, the Place of Truth","Maulana Karenga, Maat, the Moral Ideal in Ancient Egypt (2004); Richard H. Wilkinson, The Complete Gods and Goddesses of Ancient Egypt (2003)"]};return f(c);})();
